; ======================================================================
;  hw_con.pi4 - the Pi 4 backend for the HwCon* seam.
;
;  THE PAYLOAD OWNS THE PIXELS AND ANVIL OWNS THE DEVICE. This file is the
;  one place that knows this board draws into a mailbox-allocated linear
;  framebuffer at 32 bits per pixel; Anvil/Hal/abi.pbi above it knows only
;  rectangles, text runs and a tier code.
;
;  It is thin over RaspberryPi4/Lib/display.pi4 (the surface: base, pitch,
;  geometry) and RaspberryPi4/Board/display.pi4 (the smooth-text
;  rasteriser and its three fonts). It re-drives nothing - the same
;  discipline hw_usb.pi4 states for the xHCI stack.
;
;  INCLUDE ORDER: after RaspberryPi4/Lib/display.pi4,
;  RaspberryPi4/Monitor/anvil_fonts.pi4, RaspberryPi4/Board/display.pi4
;  (DrawSmoothText, SmoothTextWidth) and RaspberryPi4/Board/screen_source.pi4
;  (ScrPresent - see THE PRESENT below), and before Anvil/Hal/abi.pbi, which
;  calls this. House rule 10: this file includes nothing itself.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE PRESENT, AND THE DAY IT WAS MISSING
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  A PAYLOAD'S FRAME HAS TO BE PRESENTED AND THIS FILE IS WHERE THAT
;  HAPPENS. HwConFrameEnd used to clean a cache line and return, which is
;  the whole of what an HDMI surface needs - the firmware scans the same
;  bytes the caller drew into - and is not the whole of what the DSI panel
;  needs on the rotation this board actually runs at.
;
;  The panel scans 800 x 1280 PORTRAIT and nothing changes that. The
;  console is used landscape, so at 90 and 270 the monitor adopts a
;  LOGICAL 1280 x 800 surface (#MON_FB_DRAW, pitch 5120) and ScrPresent
;  transposes the rows that changed into the portrait buffer the scaler
;  reads (#MON_FB_SCAN, pitch 3200). Every drawing primitive in this file
;  writes into the adopted surface, which is the logical one - correctly -
;  and until today NOTHING MOVED THOSE BYTES TO THE PANEL for a payload.
;
;  What that cost: a UI payload ran four times returning 0, having drawn
;  a complete and correctly laid out frame, with the panel showing either
;  the console it had replaced or a turned mixture of the two. Every
;  number said the run was clean. The pixels never left the back buffer.
;
;  SO THE PAYLOAD'S FRAME CONTRACT IS THE SAME AS THE TOUCH GROUP'S, IN
;  THE OTHER DIRECTION. The touch seam hands a payload SCREEN pixels
;  "already turned and scaled, so no payload ever learns which way up the
;  panel is bolted"; a payload that had to learn the panel's rotation to
;  put a pixel back would be the same fact discovered twice, in two
;  places, with two chances to disagree. The payload draws in LOGICAL
;  screen coordinates - HwConWidth() by HwConHeight() - and the BOARD
;  turns them. There is no rotation, no pitch and no physical geometry
;  anywhere in the published console group, deliberately.
;
;  IT IS DAMAGE-DRIVEN, for the same reason the console's own present is.
;  A transpose of the whole panel is 1,024,000 pixels through a processor
;  loop with the caches off; a UI that repaints one band should pay for
;  one band. HwConRect and HwConText widen the band, HwConFrameBegin
;  clears it, HwConFrameEnd presents exactly it. A frame that drew nothing
;  presents nothing rather than a screenful of unchanged pixels.
;
;  AT ROTATION 0 AND 180, AND ON HDMI, ScrPresent RETURNS ON A COMPARISON
;  and the cost is the cache maintenance alone - the bytes drawn are the
;  bytes scanned. There is no flag here and no second path: one call,
;  which the seam below the console knows how to make cheap.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHY THE PIXELS ARE WRITTEN BY A PLAIN LOOP AND NOT BY THE DMA ENGINE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The monitor's own console scrolls with DMA channel 0 and RunAt takes
;  that channel back on every return, because "a payload must not leave a
;  bus master running underneath the monitor" (cache.pi4). A payload
;  filling rectangles through the same channel would be the mirror of that
;  problem - two owners, one engine, no agreement - and the monitor is not
;  running to arbitrate while the payload has the machine.
;
;  A processor loop needs no arbitration, cannot be left running, and is
;  fast enough for a UI that redraws rectangles rather than whole screens.
;  HwConTier() reports #HW_TIER_CPU honestly rather than claiming a tier
;  this path does not use, so a payload that measures its frame cost knows
;  what it measured.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE ONE HONEST LIMITATION, SAID OUT LOUD RATHER THAN DISCOVERED
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THIS BOARD HAS THREE FONTS AND ONE SIZE. anvil_fonts.pi4 carries sans,
;  serif and condensed, each rasterised at a single line height (57, 58
;  and 57 pixels). The ABI's SvcText takes a pixel height because a UI
;  wants one; this backend cannot honour it, so HwConTextHeight() reports
;  the height the board actually draws at and a payload that lays out
;  against the number it asked for rather than the number it got will draw
;  outside its own boxes.
;
;  Scaled or multi-size text is real work (a second rasteriser, or a
;  distance field) and it is not Phase A. Reporting the truth costs one
;  procedure; pretending would cost somebody an afternoon in front of a
;  screen wondering why the rows overlap.
; ======================================================================

; The tier vocabulary. In Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi's portable set, restated here
; only in the comment: #HW_TIER_V3D / _DMA / _CPU.

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE DAMAGE BAND - logical rows, inclusive, for the frame now open.
;
;  hwConDmg = 0 means NOTHING WAS DRAWN, which is a different state from
;  "row 0 was drawn" and has to be, or every empty frame would transpose
;  a row nobody touched. The band is in the same logical coordinates the
;  caller draws in, because that is what ScrPresent takes.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global hwConDmg.i
Global hwConDmgY0.i
Global hwConDmgY1.i

; Widen the band by logical rows y .. y + h - 1. Called from every
; primitive that puts a pixel down, and from nowhere else; a primitive
; that draws without calling this is a band of the screen that stops
; updating and nothing in the drawing code is wrong.
Procedure HwConDamage(y.i, h.i)
  Define y1.i
  If h <= 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  y1 = y + h - 1
  If y < 0
    y = 0
  EndIf
  If y1 < y
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If hwConDmg = 0
    hwConDmg = 1
    hwConDmgY0 = y
    hwConDmgY1 = y1
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If y < hwConDmgY0
    hwConDmgY0 = y
  EndIf
  If y1 > hwConDmgY1
    hwConDmgY1 = y1
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Geometry. Straight from the surface the mailbox allocated.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwConWidth()
  ProcedureReturn DisplayWidth()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwConHeight()
  ProcedureReturn DisplayHeight()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwConTier()
  ProcedureReturn #HW_TIER_CPU
EndProcedure

; The single line height this board's fonts are rasterised at. See the
; limitation note above.
Procedure.i HwConTextHeight()
  ProcedureReturn #FONT_sans_LINEH
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwConFrameBegin / HwConFrameEnd
;
;  THERE IS NO BACK BUFFER ON THIS PATH AND THE SEAM SAYS SO. The
;  monitor's V3D console can double-buffer; this direct path draws
;  straight into the visible surface, so Begin only checks that a surface
;  exists and End is where the cost is measured.
;
;  Begin returns 1 when there is a surface to draw on, 0 when there is
;  not - which on this board means the mailbox never gave the monitor a
;  framebuffer, and a payload that drew anyway would be writing to
;  address 0.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwConFrameBegin()
  If DisplayReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If DisplayBase() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; AND THERE HAS TO BE A ROUTE TO THE GLASS, NOT ONLY A SURFACE. A
  ; payload that drew a complete frame into a buffer nothing scans and
  ; returned cleanly is the failure that cost four board runs; the
  ; screen is the whole of what a UI has to say, so it is refused here
  ; rather than discovered by somebody watching a dark panel. The
  ; question is asked in screen_source.pi4, which owns the answer.
  If ScrPresentable() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; A NEW FRAME HAS DRAWN NOTHING YET. The band left over from the last
  ; one would present rows this frame never touched, which is free on a
  ; correct picture and is exactly how a stale row survives a redraw.
  hwConDmg = 0
  hwConDmgY0 = 0
  hwConDmgY1 = 0
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; THIS IS THE PAYLOAD'S PRESENT. See THE PRESENT in the header for what
; it is and for the day it was not here.
;
; The cache maintenance covers the rows that were DRAWN, in the surface
; that was drawn into; ScrPresent then moves those rows onto the panel
; and does its own maintenance on the bytes it writes there. At rotation
; 0 and 180 and on HDMI the two surfaces are the same bytes and
; ScrPresent returns on a comparison, so the flush below is the whole of
; what those paths need - which is why it is here and not inside the
; rotation case.
Procedure.i HwConFrameEnd()
  Define h.i
  If DisplayReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If hwConDmg = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If hwConDmgY0 < 0
    hwConDmgY0 = 0
  EndIf
  If hwConDmgY1 > DisplayHeight() - 1
    hwConDmgY1 = DisplayHeight() - 1
  EndIf
  If hwConDmgY1 < hwConDmgY0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  h = hwConDmgY1 - hwConDmgY0 + 1
  DisplayFlushRect(0, hwConDmgY0, DisplayWidth(), h)
  ScrPresent(hwConDmgY0, hwConDmgY1)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwConRect(x, y, w, h, argb) - a filled, alpha-blended rectangle.
;
;  THE ONE PRIMITIVE THE WHOLE UI IS BUILT FROM. The V3D study measured
;  that GlIdeShell makes zero direct draw calls and reduces to a blended
;  rect, a tinted textured quad and a clip; the ELD's screens are the
;  same shape.
;
;  THE CALLER HAS ALREADY CLIPPED. Anvil/Hal/abi.pbi's SvcRect intersects
;  against the scissor and against the screen before calling here, because
;  rectangle arithmetic is not silicon knowledge and belongs where it can
;  be tested without a board. This procedure still refuses a range it
;  cannot draw - a second check on the one path that would otherwise write
;  outside the surface, which is the class of bug that corrupts something
;  else entirely and is diagnosed hours later.
;
;  ALPHA IS THE TOP BYTE. 255 is opaque and takes the fast path with no
;  read at all; 0 draws nothing; anything between reads the destination
;  and blends per channel. Blending in integers, 0..255, with the
;  round-to-nearest term - (a*s + (255-a)*d + 127) / 255 - because
;  dropping it darkens every translucent overlay by up to one level per
;  composite, and a UI composites the same panel every frame.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwConRect(x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i, argb.i)
  Define base.i
  Define pitch.i
  Define sw.i
  Define sh.i
  Define a.i
  Define sr.i
  Define sg.i
  Define sb.i
  Define row.i
  Define col.i
  Define p.i
  Define d.i
  Define dr.i
  Define dg.i
  Define db.i

  If DisplayReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  base = DisplayBase()
  If base = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If w <= 0 Or h <= 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  sw = DisplayWidth()
  sh = DisplayHeight()
  If x < 0
    w = w + x
    x = 0
  EndIf
  If y < 0
    h = h + y
    y = 0
  EndIf
  If x + w > sw
    w = sw - x
  EndIf
  If y + h > sh
    h = sh - y
  EndIf
  If w <= 0 Or h <= 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  a = (argb >> 24) & $FF
  If a = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  sr = (argb >> 16) & $FF
  sg = (argb >> 8) & $FF
  sb = argb & $FF

  ; The band is widened AFTER the clamp above, so it names rows that were
  ; really written and never rows the clamp threw away.
  HwConDamage(y, h)

  pitch = DisplayPitch()
  row = 0
  While row < h
    p = base + (y + row) * pitch + x * 4
    col = 0
    While col < w
      If a = 255
        PokeL(p, argb & $00FFFFFF)
      Else
        d = PeekL(p)
        dr = (d >> 16) & $FF
        dg = (d >> 8) & $FF
        db = d & $FF
        dr = (a * sr + (255 - a) * dr + 127) / 255
        dg = (a * sg + (255 - a) * dg + 127) / 255
        db = (a * sb + (255 - a) * db + 127) / 255
        PokeL(p, (dr << 16) | (dg << 8) | db)
      EndIf
      p = p + 4
      col = col + 1
    Wend
    row = row + 1
  Wend
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwConText(x, y, *utf8, argb, bg) - one run of text, baseline at y.
;
;  y IS THE BASELINE AND NOT THE TOP, because that is what the rasteriser
;  underneath takes and because a UI that aligns two runs of different
;  glyphs aligns their baselines. HwConTextHeight() and the font's ascent
;  are what a caller converts a top-left box into a baseline with.
;
;  THE BACKGROUND IS A PARAMETER AND NOT AN ALPHA. DrawSmoothText blends
;  each glyph's coverage between fg and bg without reading the surface,
;  which is what makes it fast and what makes it wrong over a picture. For
;  the ELD's screens - flat panels of colour - passing the panel's own
;  colour is exactly right, and it is the caller who knows what that is.
;
;  Returns the width drawn.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE BAND A RUN OF TEXT DAMAGES IS DELIBERATELY GENEROUS AND SAYS SO.
; y is the baseline, the face has an ascent above it and a descent below,
; and the console's magnification can make both larger than the unscaled
; metric this file reports. A band one whole line height above the
; baseline and half of one below covers the scaled case with room, and
; the cost of being wrong in the two directions is not symmetrical: a few
; tens of extra rows in a transpose are microseconds, and a row of
; letters that never reaches the panel is a screen a driver reads as
; broken. It is a DAMAGE estimate, not a metric, and nothing lays out
; against it.
Procedure.i HwConText(x.i, y.i, *utf8, argb.i, bg.i)
  Define lh.i
  If DisplayReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If *utf8 = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  lh = HwConTextHeight()
  HwConDamage(y - lh, lh + lh / 2 + 1)
  ProcedureReturn DrawSmoothText(x, y, *utf8, 0, argb & $00FFFFFF, bg & $00FFFFFF)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwConTextWidth(*utf8)
  If *utf8 = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn SmoothTextWidth(*utf8, 0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwConBacklight(pct)
;
;  -1, AND THAT IS THE HONEST ANSWER RATHER THAN A NO-OP THAT RETURNS 0.
;  An HDMI monitor's brightness is the monitor's own, reachable only over
;  DDC/CI and not at all through this board's framebuffer path. A payload
;  that dimmed the screen for a night-driving mode and got a silent
;  success would show the driver a screen that never dims and no reason.
;  The DSI panel has a real backlight over the mailbox, and when that
;  backend exists it answers here.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwConBacklight(pct.i)
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwConCapture() - KEEP THE FRAME THE PANEL IS SHOWING.
;
;  THE PAYLOAD'S OWN WAY TO ASK FOR A PICTURE. A payload that draws
;  something worth keeping calls the console group's capture slot when it
;  has finished presenting; this is what that slot reaches. It is the
;  same copy the operator's `shot now` makes and the same copy the
;  monitor takes on a payload's way back, so there is one shape of
;  capture in the area and one decoder on the host.
;
;  IT CAPTURES WHAT THE PANEL IS SCANNING, NOT WHAT THE PAYLOAD DREW
;  INTO. On this board with the console sideways those are two different
;  buffers, and the one that matters is the one the glass is reading -
;  see ScrCaptureToArea in RaspberryPi4/Board/screen_source.pi4 for why a
;  capture of the drawing buffer would be a picture of an intention.
;
;  IT IS AFTER THE PRESENT, NOT INSTEAD OF ONE. This copies; it does not
;  flip, drain or damage anything. A payload that calls it without having
;  ended its frame keeps whatever was last presented, which is the honest
;  answer to the question it asked.
;
;  1 on a complete capture, 0 when there is no screen behind the console
;  or its surface will not fit the area. The seam returns a boolean and
;  the ABI turns it into a code; the reason a capture was refused is a
;  property of the board and `shot status` prints it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwConCapture()
  ProcedureReturn ScrShotNow(#MON_SHOT_WHO_PAYLOAD, 0)
EndProcedure
